// ============================================================================
== 6.1 What a Lexis Question Actually Asks

Three question types look alike and are marked differently. A *synonym* question asks for a word that means the same thing in that sentence. An *antonym* question asks for the opposite. A *substitution* question gives you a sentence with one word underlined and asks which option could replace it without changing the meaning.

#concept-box("The three question types")[
  + *Synonym:* “The word *X* is nearest in meaning to …” — the answer is close in meaning, not identical.
  + *Antonym:* “The word *X* is opposite in meaning to …” — the answer is the other end of the same scale.
  + *Substitution:* “Choose the option that best replaces the underlined word.” — the answer must fit the sentence grammatically as well as in meaning.

  In all three, the sentence is the authority. A word that is a perfect synonym in the dictionary can be wrong in the sentence, because synonyms differ in strength, in register and in the company they keep.
]

// ============================================================================
== 6.2 Synonyms in Context

Two words are synonyms when they can replace each other in a sentence without changing the meaning. In practice they rarely can, because synonyms differ in strength — and the paper builds its distractors out of exactly that difference.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_synonym_scale.svg",
  "Synonyms are points on a scale, not copies. The right answer is the point that matches the strength and register of the original, which is why the strongest word is usually a distractor.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Four tests before you choose a synonym")[
  + *Put it back into the sentence.* If the sentence now means something different, the option is wrong.
  + *Check the strength.* *Angry*, *furious* and *incensed* are not interchangeable; match the degree.
  + *Check the register.* *Commence* is formal, *start* is neutral, *kick off* is informal. A formal passage takes a formal synonym.
  + *Check the grammar.* A synonym must be the same part of speech and take the same preposition: *depend on* needs a synonym that also takes *on*.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The nearest meaning, not the dictionary meaning",
  [“The teacher's remark was *candid*, and some parents found it uncomfortable.” The word *candid* is nearest in meaning to: (a) rude (b) honest (c) careless (d) secret],
  [
    *Step 1 — define the word in its sentence.* *Candid* means frank and open — saying what one actually thinks.

    *Step 2 — test each option.* *Rude* means impolite, which candidness can cause but does not mean. *Careless* means without attention. *Secret* is the opposite of candid. *Honest* is the same idea.

    *Step 3 — confirm with the sentence.* The remark made parents uncomfortable because it was honest, not because it was rude; the discomfort is a consequence, which is exactly how a distractor is built.

    *Check.* Put *honest* into the sentence: “the teacher's remark was honest, and some parents found it uncomfortable.” The meaning survives, so *honest* is the answer.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#trap-box("Same topic is not the same meaning")[
  The commonest wrong answer in a synonym question belongs to the same *topic* as the word rather than having the same *meaning*. *Hospital* is not a synonym of *doctor*; *classroom* is not a synonym of *teacher*; *rain* is not a synonym of *cloud*. The test is substitution: if the sentence collapses when you drop the word in, the option is a neighbour and not a synonym.
]

// ============================================================================
== 6.3 Antonyms

An opposite is not always the other end of a line. Some opposites exclude each other completely, some sit at the two ends of a scale with a middle, and some are opposites only because they name the two roles in one relationship.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_antonyms.svg",
  "Three kinds of opposite. The question decides which kind it wants, and a complementary opposite offered where a gradable one is needed is a classic distractor.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Choosing the right kind of opposite")[
  + *Gradable* — hot and cold, with warm and cool between. Used with *very*, *rather*, *quite*.
  + *Complementary* — alive and dead, married and single. There is no middle, so one excludes the other.
  + *Relational* — buy and sell, teacher and pupil, parent and child. Each is defined by the other's role.
  + Some words have *no* exact opposite: *a table*, *a river*, *blue*. The paper will not ask for one, but it will offer near-opposites that are merely different.
]

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The opposite of a word in a sentence",
  [“The committee's decision was *unanimous*.” The word opposite in meaning to *unanimous* is: (a) divided (b) final (c) secret (d) delayed],
  [
    *Step 1 — define the word.* *Unanimous* means everyone agreed: one voice, no dissent.

    *Step 2 — look for the other end of the same scale.* The scale is agreement versus disagreement, and its other end is *divided* — some for, some against.

    *Step 3 — eliminate the neighbours.* *Final*, *secret* and *delayed* describe other qualities of a decision, not its degree of agreement. They are topic neighbours, not opposites.

    *Check.* Substitute *divided*: “the committee's decision was divided” is the exact contradiction of the original, which is what “opposite in meaning” requires.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

// ============================================================================
== 6.4 Word Substitution

A substitution question gives a sentence and asks which option can replace one word. The answer must fit the sentence in meaning *and* in grammar — a word that means the right thing but takes a different preposition is still wrong.

#shortcut-box("The substitution drill")[
  + Read the sentence without the word and decide what it must mean.
  + Put each option into the gap and read the whole sentence aloud.
  + Reject any option that changes the tense, the number or the preposition.
  + Prefer the plainest option that keeps the meaning: the paper's distractors are usually more colourful than the key.
]

#worked-example(
  3,
  "A substitution that must fit the grammar",
  [“The manager *refused* to comment on the matter.” Which option best replaces *refused*? (a) declined (b) rejected (c) denied (d) opposed],
  [
    *Step 1 — what does the sentence need?* A verb that means “said no to an invitation to speak” and that takes *to* plus the infinitive.

    *Step 2 — test the grammar.* *Declined to comment* is correct. *Rejected to comment* is not English — *reject* takes a direct object. *Denied to comment* is also ungrammatical. *Opposed to comment* changes the meaning to disagreement about commenting.

    *Step 3 — confirm the meaning.* *Declined* carries the same politeness and the same meaning as *refused* in this context.

    *Check.* Every option is roughly in the right semantic field; only one can be followed by *to* plus a verb. When meaning does not separate them, grammar does.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

// ============================================================================
== 6.5 Confusables, Idioms and Register

The last group of lexis questions is memory work, and it is the easiest place in the paper to gain marks quickly because the list is short and the same pairs return.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_confusables.svg",
  "The twelve confusable pairs the paper returns to. Learn each pair together, because the question always sets one against the other.", width: 100%)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_idioms.svg",
  "Idioms and phrasal verbs in the form they are tested. The third column is the literal reading, and it is always one of the options.", width: 100%)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_register.svg",
  "Register: grammatical but still wrong. A slangy or translated option is almost never the key, however well it fits the sense.", width: 100%)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "An idiom read literally",
  [“The chairman decided to *bury the hatchet* with his old rival.” The expression means to: (a) hide a weapon (b) end a quarrel (c) dig a grave (d) postpone a meeting],
  [
    *Step 1 — recognise the expression.* *Bury the hatchet* is a fixed idiom, so its meaning is not built from its words.

    *Step 2 — use the sentence.* The chairman is with an *old rival*, and the verb is *decided to*. Ending a quarrel fits the situation; hiding a weapon does not.

    *Step 3 — eliminate the literal readings.* (a) and (c) take the words at face value, which is exactly the trap. (d) invents a postponement that the sentence does not support.

    *Check.* Replace the idiom with its meaning: “the chairman decided to end the quarrel with his old rival.” The sentence still makes sense, so the meaning is right.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "A pair that sounds alike",
  [“The new policy will \_\_\_ the way the department works, and the \_\_\_ will be felt for years.” Options: affect / effect, effect / affect, affect / affect, effect / effect],
  [
    *Step 1 — the first blank.* It follows *will* and takes an object — *the way the department works* — so it is a verb: *affect*.

    *Step 2 — the second blank.* It follows *the* and is the subject of *will be felt*, so it is a noun: *effect*.

    *Step 3 — fix the pair in memory.* *Affect* is the action (both begin with *a*); *effect* is the end result (both begin with *e*).

    *Check.* Read the sentence with the definitions: “the new policy will influence the way the department works, and the result will be felt for years.” Both words are doing the job the sentence needs.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

