#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 6 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    A lexis question asks for meaning, and the sentence is the authority: a synonym must fit the sentence in strength, register and grammar, not merely in the dictionary. Same-topic words — doctor for hospital, rain for cloud — are the commonest distractors, and stronger words are the second commonest. Opposites are gradable, complementary or relational, and the question decides which kind it wants. A substitution must fit the sentence grammatically as well as semantically: *declined to comment* is right where *rejected to comment* is not. The twelve confusable pairs — affect and effect, principal and principle, stationary and stationery, its and it's — are pure memory work and worth an hour. An idiom is learned as one unit, and its literal reading is always offered as an option. Register is the final filter: formal writing takes formal words, and a direct translation from a Nigerian language is almost never the key.
  ]
]

== 6.6 Chapter 6 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [The word *frugal* in the sentence “He led a frugal life” is nearest in meaning to], [poor], [thrifty], [miserable], [simple])
#mcq(2, [The word opposite in meaning to *generous* is], [wealthy], [kind], [mean], [careful])
#mcq(3, [The expression *to beat about the bush* means to], [search the garden], [avoid the point], [speak loudly], [work slowly])
#mcq(4, [The new rule will \_\_\_ every member of staff, and its \_\_\_ will be felt at once.], [effect / affect], [affect / effect], [affect / affect], [effect / effect])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "C", "B", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [frugal means careful with money, which is thrifty. Poor names a lack of money rather than a habit with it, and simple describes the life rather than the spending.]),
  ("C", [generous is about giving, and its opposite is mean or stingy. Wealthy is about having, not giving; careful is close to frugal and not an opposite.]),
  ("B", [the idiom means to avoid coming to the point. Searching the garden is the literal reading, which the paper always offers as a trap.]),
  ("B", [affect is the verb and effect the noun: the rule affects staff, and its effect is felt. The pair runs verb then noun in this sentence.]),
))

